### Second step: a process that runs beside its parent

```
CapProcess: ... {
  mut .start(main: Main, attenuators: List[SystemAttenuator]): mut RunningMain;
  }
RunningMain: ToIso[RunningMain], WidenTo[RunningMain]{
  mut .await: Void;
  mut .awaitUpTo(millis: Nat): Bool;
  mut .terminate: Void;
  }
```
`.start` starts the process and returns at once. `.await` ends as `.run` ends. `.awaitUpTo` waits up to the time given and tells whether the process ended; a process that ended is then awaited with `.await`, which ends at once. `.terminate` ends the process and every process it started; `.await` on a terminated process is a non deterministic error. A method that can fail only because of the environment (`.start`, `.terminate`) throws a non deterministic error, as `.run` does.
Timeouts become deterministic from the point of view of the program: `start`, `awaitUpTo`, `terminate` is a computation whose result is the main completing or the main cut; the cut is clean because the process boundary is where revocation is deep.

### Third step: values across processes

An `Info` is the one value with a text form that parses back, so it is the first value to cross:
```
InfoMain: CaptureFree { .main(sys: mut System, arg: Info): Info }
CapProcess: ... {
  mut .run(main: InfoMain, arg: Info, attenuators: List[SystemAttenuator]): Info;
  }
```
The argument travels to the new process with the names, the result travels back with the `ok` line. Any immutable value with `ToInfo` and a parser from `Info` crosses this way; a general `imm` transfer needs a serialization protocol for every immutable type, which Fearless does not have yet, and which `DataType.info` is a start of.

### Related work and the paper

Object capabilities: an object capability is an object that can do a privileged thing, and the reference to it is the permission (Miller, Robust Composition, 2006; Miller, Yee, Shapiro, Capability Myths Demolished, 2003). Attenuation, the caretaker (a forwarder with a gate that revokes it) and the membrane (a forwarder wrapping everything that passes through it) are patterns of that literature, in E and in JavaScript (Miller; Van Cutsem and Miller on membranes); `CaptureFree` plays the role of the `DeepFrozen` auditor of E, the guarantee that a value is closed.
Capability safety of languages, and reasoning about what code can do from the capabilities it holds: Maffeis, Mitchell and Taly (Object capabilities and isolation of untrusted web applications, 2010) formalize capability safety for JavaScript subsets; Drossopoulou, Noble and Miller (The need for capability policies, 2013; Swapsies on the Internet, 2015) and Drossopoulou, Noble, Mackay and Eisenbach (holistic specifications) specify what an object capability guarantees against untrusted code; Devriese, Birkedal and Piessens (Reasoning about object capabilities with logical relations and effect parametricity, 2016) prove authority bounds from types; Melicher, Shi, Potanin and Aldrich (A capability-based module system for authority control, 2017) make modules of Wyvern receive their authority as capabilities. Gordon et al. (Uniqueness and reference immutability for safe parallelism, 2012) is the reference capability side that Fearless builds on: `iso`, `mut`, `read`, `imm` are reference capabilities, and `System` is an object capability; the attenuation design is where the two meet, since reconstruction rests on `CaptureFree` and Observable Determinism, which are typing facts.
Erlang spawns by module, function and arguments rather than by closure for the same reason reconstruction works here: a name travels, a closure does not. Fearless adds that the function named is pure by typing, so the spawned process is known to reach the same authority its parent describes.
What is new: in the object capability literature authority crosses a boundary by transferring a reference; here nothing is transferred, the new process reconstructs its authority from names, and the reconstruction is correct because the type system makes the construction a deterministic computation. Observable Determinism turns a description (a list of type names) into a faithful account of an object graph (the attenuated `System`): reconstruction is as good as transfer, and cheaper, since a name is portable and an object is not.
The claim to make: attenuation composed by reconstruction gives capability attenuation that is at the same time dynamic (chosen per run, per main, by the user or by the calling main) and statically known (the names are the whole policy), across operating system processes, with no serialization of capabilities.
